<section class="section"><div class="container blog-detail"><img class="cover reveal" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769029259587-6821e9648570?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=82" alt="Understanding Minimally Invasive Cancer Surgery" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><div class="eyebrow">PATIENT EDUCATION</div><h2>What patients should know</h2><p>What laparoscopic and robotic approaches are designed to do, and why suitability is individualized. This article is designed as general educational information and should not replace individualized medical advice.</p><h3>Questions to discuss</h3><p>Ask about the diagnosis, stage, treatment options, expected benefits, alternatives, risks, recovery timeline and follow-up plan. Bring relevant imaging, pathology reports and medication information where available.</p><h3>Every treatment plan is individual</h3><p>Surgical decisions depend on the cancer type, stage, anatomy, overall health and the wider oncology plan. The appropriate approach should be discussed directly with the treating team.</p><div class="cta-band" style="margin-top:35px;border-radius:22px"><div class="container cta-inner"><h2>Need a surgical oncology consultation?</h2><a class="btn btn-pink" href="../contact.php">Book Appointment ↗</a></div></div></div></section>
